---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { md, esc } from "../lib/md.js";
import sr from "../data/searched.json";
import Searched from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Searched.astro";
import nf from "../data/namefold.json";
const sets = sr.sets;
const rows = sets.reduce((n, s) => n + s.rows.length, 0);
const nothing = sets.reduce((n, s) => n + s.rows.filter(r => r[3] === "no").length, 0);
const found = sets.reduce((n, s) => n + s.rows.filter(r => r[3] === "yes").length, 0);
const badge = { yes: ["Found", "doc"], part: ["Partly", "inf"], no: ["Nothing", "lore"],
                void: ["Void", "open"], blocked: ["Blocked", "open"] };
/* ONE INDEX OVER ALL 606 QUERIES, BESIDE THE SESSIONS THAT RAN THEM.
 *
 * The sections below are the narrative: seven queries put in one sitting under
 * one headline, and why. That is worth keeping and no table replaces it. What
 * it could not do is ANSWER A QUESTION ABOUT ITSELF - has anybody searched for
 * this, and what came back - because finding out meant scrolling 112 sections.
 *
 * Both are drawn from the same rows, so they cannot disagree.
 *
 * WORK ITEM 94 SAID THIS DATA HAD NO OUTCOME FIELD ON ANY OF IT. It has one on
 * every one of 606 rows, and had one the day before that was written: the
 * fourth column, yes / no / part / blocked / void. The row had read the shape
 * of a SET - g, n, d, h, rows - and never opened a row.
 *
 * `nil` is ["no"] alone. BLOCKED IS NOT NOTHING: a paywall and a dead server
 * mean the search never ran, and counting them as empty would turn thirteen
 * unasked questions into thirteen answers. VOID is not a search at all - a bare
 * surname returning 552 documents, a memoir that turned out unnecessary. */
/* THE NOTE AND THE RESULT ARE WRITTEN IN THE ARCHIVE'S MARKUP AND WERE PRINTED
 * AS ASTERISKS. Measured 9 October 2026 on a build into site/dist-base: 2,428
 * literal `**` in this page's visible text against 1,564 <strong>, and the
 * asterisks were the data's own emphasis arriving unrendered — 601 bold pairs,
 * 36 italics, 7 internal links, every one of them twice because the rows are
 * drawn once into the index above and once into the sessions below.
 *
 * THE QUERY COLUMN IS DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, and that is the whole reason
 * `esc` is still here beside `md`. Twelve of its asterisks are NAAIRS and
 * FamilySearch wildcards, not emphasis -
 *     q.surname=B*rry · q.motherSurname=Kolb* and Col*
 * - and three of those flank a letter on both sides, so a renderer pairs them
 * into an italic running across two surnames and silently rewrites the query
 * somebody would re-run to check this archive. One query does carry a real
 * italic (a book title) and it stays an asterisk; that is the cheaper error.
 * The note and result columns were counted the same way first: of their 170
 * single asterisks exactly one is a wildcard - `BOOY* in the Cape depot
 * returns 0 documents` - and md() leaves it alone, because a delimiter only
 * opens a span when the next character is not a space. */
const flat = sets.flatMap(x => x.rows.map(([q, res, note, k]) => ({
  when: x.d, src: x.g, what: "<strong class=\"m\">" + esc(q) + "</strong>",
  got: md(res), extra: md(note), outcome: k, href: x.h,
})));
---
<Base title="What Has Been Read"
      eyebrow={`${rows} queries · ${nothing} returned nothing · ${found} found something`}
      dek={sr.note}>

<section class="blk">
  <div class="note">
    <strong>Why a page of failures.</strong> Most family history records only what was found. That makes
    it impossible to tell the difference between <em>“there is nothing there”</em> and <em>“nobody has
    looked”</em> — and those are completely different statements about an ancestor. Everything below was
    actually run, and the wording of each query is given so it can be run again and checked.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Every query, in one list</h2>
  <p class="dek">The sessions below are the story of the work — what was asked in one
    sitting, and why. This is the index: <b>all {flat.length} queries at once</b>, so the page
    can answer a question about itself. Both are drawn from the same rows.</p>
  <Searched rows={flat} u={u} nil={["no"]} unit="queries" fold={nf.fold} />
</section>

{sets.map((s, i) => (
  <>
    <Rule name={["koppie", "thorn", "windpump", "gable", "awl", "wagon"][i % 6]} />
    <section class="blk">
      <h2>{s.g}</h2>
      <p class="dek">{s.n} · searched {s.d} · <a href={u(s.h)}>where it is used &rarr;</a></p>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">Query</th><th class="m" style="width:8rem">Result</th><th></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {s.rows.map(([q, res, note, k]) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong class="m">{q}</strong></td>
                <td class="m"><Fragment set:html={md(res)} /><div style="margin-top:5px"><span class={"chip " + badge[k][1]}>{badge[k][0]}</span></div></td>
                <td set:html={md(note)} />
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  </>
))}

<Rule name="koppie" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the zeros are worth</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>A zero with a stated scope is evidence.</b> There is no estate file for Hercules Johannes
      Mountjoy in the Cape archives, and because the estates on either side of him run unbroken from 1938
      to 1948, that absence means something: an estate too small to report.</p>
    <p><b>A zero without a scope is not.</b> The camp database returns nothing for this family — but it is
      built largely from camp <em>death</em> lists, so it records who died far better than who was interned
      and walked out. That claim is <em>unsupported</em>, not disproved, and the page says so.</p>
    <p><b>And a zero from the wrong index is worth less than nothing</b>, because it can mislead for years.
      The Farm List result above is void: the register is an Orange Free State land roll and was never
      going to hold a Cape farm. It took a control search to notice.</p>
    <blockquote>The searches that found something are on the other pages. These are the ones that make
      those worth believing.</blockquote>
    <p><a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What is still open &rarr;</a> ·
       <a href={u("/method/")}>How this archive works &rarr;</a> ·
       <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
